# 2. BRAND IDENTITY AND DESIGN SYSTEM

## 2.1 Primary Colours

Use the following colour palette:

| Colour | Hex Code | Application |
|---|---|---|
| Deep Black | #080808 | Primary background |
| Soft Black | #111111 | Secondary sections and cards |
| Antique Gold | #C5A15A | Primary accent, headings, borders |
| Champagne Gold | #D8C18A | Secondary highlights |
| Warm White | #F5F2EB | Primary text on dark backgrounds |
| Muted Grey | #A6A29A | Secondary text |

Black must dominate the website. Gold should be used selectively for emphasis, visual hierarchy, borders, navigation details, and interactive states.

Avoid excessive gold backgrounds or large areas of bright yellow. The gold should resemble brushed or antique gold rather than a bright metallic yellow.

Some sections may introduce a warm ivory or off-white background to create contrast and give the website an editorial, luxury-law-firm feel. However, black should remain the dominant colour across the complete website.

## 2.2 Typography

Use an elegant serif typeface for major headings and a clean sans-serif typeface for body text.

Recommended combinations:

- Headings: Cormorant Garamond, Playfair Display, or Libre Baskerville.
- Body text: Inter, Manrope, or Montserrat.

Typography should communicate authority, sophistication, and tradition.

Use:
- Large editorial-style headings.
- Generous spacing between paragraphs.
- Carefully controlled line lengths.
- Subtle uppercase section labels.
- Gold highlights for selected words or phrases.

Avoid overly decorative fonts.

## 2.3 Visual Direction

The website should draw inspiration from premium international law firms, luxury editorial publications, and sophisticated corporate websites.

Visual characteristics:
- Generous negative space.
- Full-width black sections.
- Thin gold divider lines.
- Subtle background textures.
- Large, carefully composed photographs.
- Asymmetrical image and text layouts.
- Minimalist icons.
- Elegant transitions between sections.
- Strong visual hierarchy.

Do not use generic law-themed stock photography excessively, particularly clichéd images of gavels, scales of justice, and courthouse columns.

